<TopicPage slug="subjects/chemistry/advanced/organic-chemistry-functional-groups">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Ten million organic compounds would be unlearnable one by one — so chemists learned to read
    them by <strong>functional groups</strong>: small clusters of atoms that behave the same
    wherever they appear. An –OH behaves like an alcohol on any skeleton; a –COOH makes any
    molecule an acid. Learn twenty groups and you can predict reactions across the entire
    subject, which is exactly the trick organic chemistry is built on.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="four groups, four personalities — each stamped onto any carbon skeleton you like" widget="equationStep" config={funcCfg} />

  <h2>Reactions as group-swaps</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Substitution:</strong> swap one group for another — the workhorse of synthesis. Soap is made by swapping fat's ester groups for salts.</li>
    <li><strong>Addition across double bonds:</strong> alkenes open their C=C and clip on new atoms — how ethene becomes polyethylene and ethylene glycol becomes antifreeze.</li>
    <li><strong>Condensation:</strong> two molecules join and kick out water — the reaction that builds proteins, nylon and polyester, step by step.</li>
    <li><strong>Oxidation ladders:</strong> alcohol → aldehyde → carboxylic acid, each step adding oxygen. Wine open too long walks this ladder to vinegar on your countertop.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Chirality: chemistry's handedness</h2>
  <p>
    A carbon with four different groups comes in mirror-image pairs — like left and right hands.
    They're chemically identical except toward other chiral things: enzymes, receptors, DNA. The
    thalidomide tragedy (one handedness treated morning sickness; the other caused birth defects)
    is why every new drug today is tested chirality by chirality.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Clayden, Greeves & Warren, Organic Chemistry; OpenStax, Organic Chemistry (Functional Groups).</p>
</TopicPage>
